Explanation of the change:

1. In the original `prepareFrame`, each layer that needed WebGL simply did  
   ```js
   this.helper = new WebGLHelper(...);
   ```
   creating a brand‐new WebGL context for every WebGL layer.

2. In the patch we instead look at `frameState.map` (the `PluggableMap` instance), and store a single shared helper instance on it under a private key (`__sharedWebGLHelper`).  
   - The first WebGL layer to run `prepareFrame` will create `map.__sharedWebGLHelper`.  
   - Every subsequent WebGL layer on the *same* map will reuse that helper – and hence the same underlying WebGL context.

With this change you can now have many WebGL tile layers on a single map without exhausting the browser’s (≈16) context limit.
